course: dictionary
lesson: 2674
title: Dialects: many ways to say it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A dialect is a form of a language spoken in one area or by one group of people. It has its own words, grammar and pronunciation, so it can sound quite different from the English in your books.
explain: In Scotland, «dreich» means dull, grey and drizzly, and in the southern US, «y'all» means you all. These are real dialect words. Context helps you work them out: a sentence about rain and grey skies tells you what dreich must mean.
explain: No dialect is wrong. Standard English is the form usually used in school writing, so a story might say «I was going to the shop», while a Lancashire gran says «I were going to't shop». Both are real English with their own rules.
explain: In a story, dialect shows where a character comes from. When a character speaks differently, ask yourself: where do they live, and what does their speech tell me about them?
fact: A dialect is a form of a language used in a particular area or by a particular group.
fact: Dialects can have their own words, grammar and pronunciation.
fact: In Scotland, «bairn» means a small child.
fact: In parts of England, «nowt» means nothing, and Americans say «sidewalk» where the British say «pavement».
fact: Different orca groups use different calls, which are also called «dialects», and they do not mix.
fact: Standard English is the form usually used in school writing.
exercise: «Local words belong to real dialects.» What is a dialect? | A very old castle | A kind of spelling test that is always wrong | *A form of a language spoken in one area or by one group
exercise: In Scotland, what does «bairn» mean? | A petrol station | *A small child | A type of boat | A grey, wet day
exercise: Which sentence is in Standard English, the form used in school writing? | I were going to't shop | Me and him was going to shop | *I was going to the shop | I going to the shop today
test: Americans say «sidewalk» where the British say «pavement». Why is this a dialect difference? | *Because the two groups use different words for the same thing | Because they speak different languages | Because one of the words is wrong | Because one of them is older
test: «Y'all» is a dialect word from the southern US. What does it mean? | Go away | Me and my friend | A small bird | *You all
exam: Is a dialect wrong? | *No, it is real English with its own rules, though school writing usually uses Standard English | No, but only if you live in Scotland | Yes, but only in stories | Yes, all dialects are wrong and must be changed
